Body:
You can add something like 

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/tui.json",
  "theme": "system",
  "plugin": ["./korean-keymap.ts"]
}
```

and define the plugin as

```ts
const layout: Record<string, string> = {
  ㅂ: "q",
  ㅃ: "q",
  ㅈ: "w",
  ㅉ: "w",
  ㄷ: "e",
  ㄸ: "e",
  ㄱ: "r",
  ㄲ: "r",
  ㅅ: "t",
  ㅆ: "t",
  ㅛ: "y",
  ㅕ: "u",
  ㅑ: "i",
  ㅐ: "o",
  ㅒ: "o",
  ㅔ: "p",
  ㅖ: "p",
  ㅁ: "a",
  ㄴ: "s",
  ㅇ: "d",
  ㄹ: "f",
  ㅎ: "g",
  ㅗ: "h",
  ㅓ: "j",
  ㅏ: "k",
  ㅣ: "l",
  ㅋ: "z",
  ㅌ: "x",
  ㅊ: "c",
  ㅍ: "v",
  ㅠ: "b",
  ㅜ: "n",
  ㅡ: "m",
  "₩": "\\",
  "￦": "\\",
};

type KeyEvent = {
  name: string;
  baseCode?: number;
  ctrl: boolean;
  shift: boolean;
  meta: boolean;
  super?: boolean;
  hyper?: boolean;
};

type MatchContext = {
  resolveKey(key: Omit<KeyEvent, "baseCode">): string;
};

type TuiApi = {
  keymap: {
    appendEventMatchResolver(
      resolve: (event: KeyEvent, ctx: MatchContext) => string[] | undefined,
    ): () => void;
  };
};

async function tui(api: TuiApi) {
  api.keymap.appendEventMatchResolver((event, ctx) => {
    if (event.baseCode !== undefined) return;
    const name = layout[event.name];
    if (!name) return;
    return [
      ctx.resolveKey({
        name,
        ctrl: event.ctrl,
        shift: event.shift,
        meta: event.meta,
        super: event.super ?? false,
        hyper: event.hyper || undefined,
      }),
    ];
  });
}

export default { id: "local.korean-keymap", tui };
```

This should be possible to remapped in `tui.json`. For a Turkish keyboard layout, you can map the install action to the key combination your terminal reports:
```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/tui.json",
  "keybinds": {
    "dialog.plugins.install": "shift+ı"
  }
}
```
Alternatively, choose a combination that your terminal distinguishes more reliably, for example:
```
{
  "keybinds": {
    "dialog.plugins.install": "ctrl+alt+i"
  }
}
```
The current TUI uses OpenTUI's generic base-layout matching when the terminal provides that information. When a terminal sends only the raw `İ` character, OpenCode cannot reliably distinguish `Shift+i` from intentional text input, Caps Lock, or IME input. Some protocols include baseCode, though. Anyway, , globally converting `İ` into `shift+i` would risk triggering the install action while typing in the search field.

If remapping doesnt work please re-open the issue.
